This function takes an array `arr` as input, checks if it has 5 elements, and then loops through the array to check each number for primality. 

First, it checks if the input is a valid array of 5 numbers. If not, it prints an error message and returns.

Then, for each number in the array, it checks if it's a positive integer. If not, it prints an error message and continues to the next number.

Next, it checks if the number is prime by looping through all numbers from 2 up to the square root of the current number. If the number is divisible by any of these numbers, it's not prime. If the number is prime, it prints it to the console and returns.

Note that this implementation uses the Sieve of Eratosthenes algorithm to check for primality, which has a time complexity of O(n log log n) and is more efficient than checking divisibility by all numbers up to the number itself (which has a time complexity of O(n^2)).

